

  <section xml:id="Math2000HW6">
    <title>National Treasure</title>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-span">
      <title>Span</title>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-heist">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            You're Nicolas Cage.
            You heard a rumor the Declaration of Independence is in the Bowdoin College Museum of Art.
            You yearn to heist, but the movie industry has weighed you down.
            All those years.
            You can only take at most <m>20</m> lbs of goods with you.
            Here are the items and their value:
          </p>

          <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="major">
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Item</cell>
              <cell>Value (each)</cell>
              <cell>Weight (each)</cell>
              <cell>Number available</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Declaration of Independence</cell>
              <cell>$1.6 million</cell>
              <cell><m>0.1</m> lbs</cell>
              <cell><m>1</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Landscape paintings</cell>
              <cell>$50</cell>
              <cell><m>2</m> lbs</cell>
              <cell><m>500</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Busts</cell>
              <cell>$300</cell>
              <cell><m>6</m> lbs</cell>
              <cell><m>3</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>

          <p>
            Answer the following questions:
          </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              What should you steal to maximize your profit?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              How much do you profit from the theft?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        We call a set of vectors in <m>\R^{n}</m> <term>spanning</term> if every vector in <m>\R^{n}</m> is in their span.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-signed-incidence">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the Fredholm alternative to argue that the set of columns of a signed incidence matrix of a network is never spanning.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        One can detect if a matrix is spanning from its reduced row echelon form.
        This happens exactly when there are number of rows many pivot columns.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-unsigned-incidence">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Characterize when the set of columns of an unsigned incidence matrix of a network is spanning in terms of the network.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-independence">
      <title>Independence</title>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-rref-columns">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Consider the following matrix:
            <me>
              A = \begin{bmatrix}
                1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \\
                1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3 &amp; 4 \\
                1 &amp; 3 &amp; 5 &amp; 7
              \end{bmatrix}
            </me>
            Its reduced row echelon form is
            <me>
              B = \begin{bmatrix}
                1 &amp; 0 &amp; -1 &amp; -2 \\
                0 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3 \\
                0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write the third column of <m>A</m> as a linear combination of the first two.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write the fourth column of <m>A</m> as a linear combination of the first two.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Explain how you could solve (a) and (b) using <m>B</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find a linearly independent subset of the columns of <m>A</m> with the same span.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-sin-cos">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the following
            <ul>
              <li><m>\sin(0) = 0</m></li>
              <li><m>\sin(\pi/2) = 1</m></li>
              <li><m>\cos(0) = 1</m></li>
              <li><m>\cos(\pi/2) = 0</m></li>
            </ul>
            to argue that <m>\sin(t)</m> and <m>\cos(t)</m> are linearly independent in the vector space of functions.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-extend-independent">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Consider a vector space <m>V</m> and a subspace <m>W</m> of <m>V</m>.
            Let <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> be linearly independent vectors in <m>W</m>.
            Let <m>\vec{w}</m> be a vector not in <m>W</m>.
            Explain why <m>\vec{u}</m>, <m>\vec{v}</m>, and <m>\vec{w}</m> are therefore linearly independent.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>
